black panther was one of marvel's most  important superheroes long before he  made his mark on the mcu but what are  t'challa's real origins and just why is  this character so beloved by comic book  fans  this is the untold truth of black  panther co-created by stan lee and jack  kirby the black panther made his debut  in 1966's fantastic four  number 52 followers of comic book  history will know that lee and kirby  didn't always agree on things but in the  case of black panther's creation both  writer and artist were in total  agreement  they knew that marvel needed the king of  wakanda to fill a void that it existed  for too long in comic books  the existence of a black superhero in a  1990 interview with the comics journal  kirby recalled realizing he wanted more  diversity in the comics he made  he said i suddenly discovered that i had  a lot of black readers and here i was  ignoring them because i was associating  with everybody else  likewise lee said he conceived black  panther with kirby because  in his own words he wanted to make the  first black superhero in a 2011  interview with alter ego lee said that  while creating black panther he was also  careful not to play into stereotypes  america was used to depictions of africa  as a backward and primitive place  so lee and kirby wanted to portray the  exact opposite lee explained  black panther is one of the greatest  scientists in the world and his area  this country is more scientifically  advanced than any when you get to the  hidden entrance and go down to wakanda  it looks like you're in a scene from a  science fiction movie of the 30th  century  when black panther first appears in  fantastic 452  his allegiances aren't exactly clear cut  he sends an emissary to new york city  with the gift of a high-tech flying  vehicle and an invitation to the team to  come to wakanda as the king's guests  upon their arrival however t'challa and  his subjects surprised the fantastic  four by attacking them  ultimately it proves to be a test black  panther hopes to get the fantastic  four's help in dealing with the villain  ulysses claw but first wants to make  sure they're all they're hyped up to be  eventually the fantastic four overcome  black panther but individually t'challa  is more than a match for any single  member of the team  he traps the human torch and easily  sniffs out the invisible susan storm  even when squaring off against the thing  black panther is able to hold his own  with nothing but his fists and his wit  in spite of their initial clashes the  fantastic four in black panther have  long maintained close ties  t'challa even took over as leader of the  team after the events of the lion wide  event civil war  it's no secret that t'challa isn't the  first black panther  his father t'chaka held the title before  him and plenty of ancestors before  t'chaka took the responsibility on their  shoulders as well and the further you go  back the more epic the black panther  struggles become  as part of marvel's legacy event from  2017  it's revealed that earth's first team of  avengers existed as far back as 1  million  bc thor's father odin leads the team  which includes prehistoric versions of  phoenix  iron fist ghost rider agamotto star  brand and  yes black panther 2. unlike t'challa the  first black panther wears the furs and  claws of actual panthers  in 2018's avengers number four odin  tells thor and she-hulk that this black  panther was the first to discover the  meteor from which wakanda harvests its  vibranium  but other than that not much is known  about this particular panther  not even his given name has been  revealed or whether he gets his  abilities from the same heart-shaped  herb as later black panthers  he's rarely been shown in action though  his dialogue reveals him to be at least  as intelligent and contemplative as  t'challa  avengers  assemble in 1999's black panther number  eight the king of wakanda drops a  serious truth bomb one that turned out  to be one of the most controversial  reveals in the marvel comics of the time  while t'challa and his avengers  colleagues try to quell a riot  mastermind by a cheby  black panther's teammates overhear him  saying he initially joined the avengers  because he wanted to see if they were a  threat to wakanda  once the smoke clears and his teammates  ask him about it t'challa confirms he  initially joined the team to spy on them  the revelation drives a wedge between  black panther and the rest of the  avengers and it's particularly damaging  to the friendship between t'challa and  steve rogers  while black panther would go on to be a  part of future avengers rosters things  have never been quite the same between  him and his super powered comrades  in 2018 writer christopher priest  addressed fan anger over the revelation  he pointed out to comicbook.com that as  the monarch of a sovereign nation going  off to have adventures with superheroes  seem like a strange choice for t'challa  priest explained i couldn't figure out  why he joined the avengers and the only  thing that made any logical sense to me  was black panther did it to find out  whether these people were a threat  once he was comfortable that they  weren't a threat he felt okay helping  them out from time to  time but his number one responsibility  was wakanda  the term graphic novel has become  popularized in recent years with some  people simply using it as a fancy  synonym for comic book  but a true graphic novel isn't just any  comic book it's a long form story  with a distinct beginning middle and end  in the 70s most superhero comics would  give you one or two part stories  but writer don mcgregor concocted what  some call marvel's very first graphic  novel and black panther was its hero  black panther's first solo series was  jungle action a title revived from  marvel's early days as atlas comics  panther's rage begins with 1973's jungle  action number six  and concludes with 1975's jungle action  18.  the story introduces eric killmonger in  his plot to replace t'challa on the  throne of wakanda and sends black  panther on the long and difficult road  to defeat his challenger  for a superhero comic to include a  13-part story spanning two years of  publication no less was basically  unheard of in the mid-70s  and doesn't even happen all that often  nowadays some have argued that it was  marvel's neglect toward lesser selling  titles that allowed for such creative  freedom  whatever the reason though you've got  t'challa to thank in part for the marvel  graphic novels that you enjoy today  t'challa is part of a long line of  warrior kings who have assumed the title  of black panther but some characters  have actually taken the name while  t'challa was still alive  more or less for example eric killmonger  defeats t'challa in one-on-one combat  during the 20th issue of 2000's black  panther run  earning him the right to become the king  of wakanda as well as the next black  panther  his reign ends in black panther 24  however when he eats the heart-shaped  herb which gives the black panthers  their powers and falls into a coma  because he isn't part of the royal  bloodline in 2002's black panther 50  after the rumored death of t'challa a  mysterious new black panther appears in  new york city  this new panther turns out to be nypd  officer casper cole  who swipes the uniform and uses it to  bring his corrupt superior officer to  justice  after t'challa reappears cole takes the  name white tiger and continues to fight  crime  even t'challa's own sister shuri became  queen of wakanda in 2009 during a story  in which t'challa falls into a coma due  to the sinister machinations of dr  doom t'challa eventually emerges from  his coma but shuri remains black panther  after that  until she sacrifices herself to save her  brother in 2014's new avengers number  24.  until the success of 2018's black  panther made more people aware of his  work writer christopher priest was a  largely unsung hero in the character's  history  despite its relatively low sales however  priests 1998-2003 run of black panther  totally redefined the character  without his work to inform it there  would be no black panther film or  at the very least it would have been a  much different and probably far less  interesting movie  as tadahasi codes another black panther  writer told vulture  he had the classic run of black panther  period and that's going to be true for a  long time  people had not put as much thought into  who and what black panther was before  christopher started writing the book  in 2018 priests told comicbook.com he  was initially hesitant to write black  panther but changed his mind when he was  told he could reinvent the characters he  saw  fit one of priest's guiding stars was  thinking of t'challa not as a superhero  but as a king  told through the eyes of t'challa's  hapless sidekick everett k ross  priest black panther is a colder and  more aloof version of the character who  places his role as king before anything  else  priest told comicbook.com he's not  allowed to think much about himself or  do much for himself his main  responsibility is wakanda he's not  really a superhero  the guy is a monarch he's following his  own agenda black panther proved to be  one of the most critically and  commercially successful  superhero films hollywood has ever made  but according to the late chadwick  bozeman black panther is more like the  godfather's michael corleone than he is  iron man or captain america  speaking to comicbook.com in late 2017  bozeman referred to black panther as an  anti-hero he said  at times black panther feels like the  godfather it's complicated when you have  to deal with who should live and who  should die  sometimes you have to do bad things or  you maybe need to do bad things so  there's justice so there's peace  fittingly bozeman's interpretation of  the character echoes the writing of  christopher priest who saw black panther  as a monarch responsible for his  people's well-being rather than a  superhero reacting to crises on the fly  bozeman even echoed spider-man's classic  mantra about great power bringing great  responsibility  the actor explained black panther is  about how you use your power what do you  do when you get power  in this case you're talking about  someone taking the throne but all  superhero movies are about a person who  has extreme power  the only difference between a hero and  the villain is that the villain chooses  to use that power in a way that is  selfish and hurts other people  the black panther film managed to work  in a number of the hero's signature  comic book bad guys  along with killmonger t'challa came up  against ulysses claw and embaku who's  known as the villain man ape in the  comics  are you done  are you are you done but while speaking  to yahoo movies in 2018 director ryan  coogler admitted there was at least one  bad guy he wanted to use but couldn't  craving the hunter coogler called  christopher priest's time on black  panther a heavy run and singled out a  fight that takes place between black  panther and the russian villain craven  as his inspiration for wanting to use  the jungle themed mercenary  cougler explained i've always loved  craven the hunter in almost every  iteration  so there was a moment can i grab craven  and they were nah you don't have craven  as he's a villain most closely  associated with spider-man sony owns the  film rights to craven and the hunter  isn't part of the deal that allows  marvel studios to use the web slinger in  their films  most likely the fight scene coogler was  referencing from priests ronaldo comics  as he impressively rendered battle  between the pair in the kitchen of the  new york hilton during 1999's black  panther number six  drawn by joe jusko the six page fight  sequence is an intense battle done  almost completely without dialogue  it's a pretty crazy scene and perfectly  encapsulates just how powerful and  innovative black panther comics can get  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies and tv shows are  coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one

